WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.006 --> 00:00:05.961
Aisha Coleman: Morning, Denise. How's everything on your side this week?

2
00:00:06.336 --> 00:00:13.961
Denise Morales: Morning, Aisha. Busy but okay. We're in the middle of our annual compliance review, so I've got binders everywhere, literally and figuratively.

3
00:00:15.271 --> 00:00:27.959
Aisha Coleman: That's the season for it. Before we get into anything, how are the call volumes looking? Last time we talked you were around twenty, twenty-two thousand a month across the member service lines.

4
00:00:29.328 --> 00:00:42.457
Denise Morales: We're a bit above that. September came in at about twenty-four six. Auto loan inquiries have been up since the rate change, and the HELOC calls never really slowed down.

5
00:00:43.895 --> 00:00:51.454
Aisha Coleman: Okay, that tracks with what I see on the call tracking side. Anything unusual happening with tracking numbers or the routing?

6
00:00:52.662 --> 00:01:00.056
Denise Morales: No, that's all been fine. The tag on the site is doing what it's supposed to. Nothing to report there.

7
00:01:00.582 --> 00:01:05.544
Aisha Coleman: Good. And the QM side, how is the team handling the volume?

8
00:01:05.804 --> 00:01:17.621
Denise Morales: That's actually what I wanted to talk about. We've got a new person starting on the quality team next Monday, and it made me think about our scorecards again.

9
00:01:18.955 --> 00:01:22.784
Aisha Coleman: Oh, good. Is that filling a vacancy or adding a seat?

10
00:01:23.227 --> 00:01:33.945
Denise Morales: Adding. We were at three reviewers and now we'll be four. Which helps, but it also means more people looking at the same templates and asking the same question.

11
00:01:35.224 --> 00:01:36.560
Aisha Coleman: What question is that?

12
00:01:36.1000 --> 00:01:51.512
Denise Morales: Whether we can build our own criteria into the scorecards. Right now we're scoring against the standard set, and it mostly works, but there are things specific to us that just don't fit anywhere.

13
00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:55.291
Aisha Coleman: Can you give me an example?

14
00:01:56.527 --> 00:02:06.817
Denise Morales: Sure. Our deposit account calls, when a member is opening a new checking account, the rep is supposed to read the funds availability disclosure and confirm the member understood it.

15
00:02:06.817 --> 00:02:12.647
Denise Morales: It's a Washington state thing for us, it's in our procedures, and the examiners ask about it.

16
00:02:13.703 --> 00:02:30.634
Denise Morales: The current scorecard has a spot for required disclosures, but it's one bucket. So a call where the rep did the funds availability piece perfectly and a call where they skipped it can end up looking the same, because they nailed the other two disclosures.

17
00:02:31.629 --> 00:02:38.256
Aisha Coleman: So you want to score that specific behavior on its own, not folded into a general disclosures category.

18
00:02:39.625 --> 00:02:55.361
Denise Morales: Exactly. And it's not just that one. We'd want to weight things differently depending on the call type. A new account call and a balance inquiry shouldn't be scored the same way, but right now they kind of are.

19
00:02:56.476 --> 00:03:04.286
Aisha Coleman: Okay. And when you say weighting, are you thinking about points, or about what counts as pass or fail, or both?

20
00:03:04.881 --> 00:03:20.373
Denise Morales: Both, honestly. If a rep misses a required disclosure, that should sink the call no matter how nice they were. But if they forgot to offer a debit card, that's a coaching note, not a failure.

21
00:03:20.961 --> 00:03:26.205
Aisha Coleman: That makes sense. How are you handling it today? Spreadsheet on the side?

22
00:03:27.497 --> 00:03:37.008
Denise Morales: You guessed it. We pull the calls, score them in the platform, then my team manually flags the disclosure misses in a spreadsheet for the review committee.

23
00:03:37.008 --> 00:03:40.882
Denise Morales: It works, but it's slow and it's not consistent between reviewers.

24
00:03:42.165 --> 00:03:47.051
Aisha Coleman: And compliance reviews every scorecard change, right? I remember that from onboarding.

25
00:03:48.269 --> 00:04:03.925
Denise Morales: Every one. Even small wording tweaks go through the committee. So whatever we do has to be documentable. If we can define criteria ourselves, I need to be able to show them exactly what changed and when.

26
00:04:04.370 --> 00:04:09.152
Aisha Coleman: Got it. So it's not just flexibility, it's the audit trail around the flexibility.

27
00:04:10.634 --> 00:04:14.519
Denise Morales: Yes. That's the part that keeps me up at night.

28
00:04:14.794 --> 00:04:26.207
Aisha Coleman: Okay. Let me write this up properly. I want to capture the disclosure example, the call-type specific scoring, and the weighting piece separately, because those might land in different places.

29
00:04:26.694 --> 00:04:32.074
Denise Morales: That's fine. I'd rather you take it back than try to solve it on the call.

30
00:04:33.223 --> 00:04:39.810
Aisha Coleman: Appreciated. Is there a timeline driving this, or is it more of a this-year wish?

31
00:04:41.014 --> 00:04:50.786
Denise Morales: Our next exam window is spring, so ideally we'd have something in place by February. But I know that's not a promise you can make.

32
00:04:52.027 --> 00:04:58.589
Aisha Coleman: Right, I won't promise anything. I'll include the February context so it's on the record.

33
00:04:59.216 --> 00:05:00.016
Denise Morales: Perfect.

34
00:05:00.688 --> 00:05:06.266
Aisha Coleman: Switching gears for a second. Who's covering the QM queues while the new hire ramps?

35
00:05:06.609 --> 00:05:16.720
Denise Morales: Rosa's taking the overflow, and we're keeping the Friday calibration meeting as is. Honestly the calibration has gotten easier since we started using the AI summaries to prep.

36
00:05:18.194 --> 00:05:27.056
Aisha Coleman: Good to hear. Anything on the Salesforce side giving you trouble? I saw your call outcomes are still writing through to Financial Services Cloud.

37
00:05:27.471 --> 00:05:33.852
Denise Morales: No, that's all been steady. The branch managers finally trust the call records, which took a while.

38
00:05:34.876 --> 00:05:36.889
Aisha Coleman: And Google Ads offline conversions?

39
00:05:38.029 --> 00:05:39.483
Denise Morales: Fine. Nothing new there.

40
00:05:39.973 --> 00:05:47.523
Aisha Coleman: Okay. I'll get the write-up over to you by Thursday, and I'll let you know what I hear back. Same time next month?

41
00:05:48.895 --> 00:05:50.710
Denise Morales: Works for me. Thanks, Aisha.

42
00:05:51.235 --> 00:05:53.819
Aisha Coleman: Thanks, Denise. Good luck with the binders.

43
00:05:54.571 --> 00:05:56.318
Denise Morales: Ha. I'll need it.
